import java.util.*;
class Main
{
   public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        payment p1=new upi();
        payment p2=new card();
        p1.pay();
        p2.pay();
    }
}
class payment
{
    void pay()
    {
    System.out.println("making payment");
    }
}
class upi extends payment
{
    void pay()
    {
        System.out.println("upi confirm");
    }       
}
class card extends payment
{
    void pay()
    {
        System.out.println("card confirm");
    }
}


explaination:-
This program demonstrates method overriding in Java.
First, I created a parent class called `Payment`, which contains a `pay()` method. Then, I created two child classes, `UpiPayment` and `CardPayment`, using the `extends` keyword.
Both child classes override the `pay()` method and provide their own implementations. In the main method, I created parent-class references pointing to child-class objects.
When I call `p1.pay()`, the UPI payment implementation executes. When I call `p2.pay()`, the card payment implementation executes.
This is an example of **runtime polymorphism**, because Java decides which overridden method to execute based on the actual object at runtime.
